{
  "id": "Compound::DB00248 - Gene::1816",
  "verbalization": "Cabergoline, a member of the ergoline class of compounds, exhibits a complex relationship with Drd5, the dopamine receptor D5, through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Cabergoline binds to Drd5 via a common molecular entity, the Nitrogen Molecular Entity, which is shared by both Cabergoline and Bromocriptine, a closely related ergoline. This binding is supported by the presence of the Dopamine Agonist ontology tag, indicating a direct interaction with dopamine signaling pathways. Additionally, Cabergoline is implicated in the pathogenesis of Pericarditis Constrictive and Excessive Daytime Sleepiness, which are associated with Bromocriptine and Rotigotine, respectively, both of which also bind to Drd5. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Cabergoline in modulating dopamine receptor activity, particularly through its interaction with Drd5. The evidence underscores a mechanistic link between Cabergoline and Drd5, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
